1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:01,413
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,413 --> 00:00:09,740
This is a short overview of GP-RareLink, a project that reads the published literature on rare genetic diseases with a language model,

3
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:14,463
turns it into structured records, and checks every value against its source.

4
00:00:14,463 --> 00:00:22,175
It is funded by Anthropic's AI for Science program and run by the Rare Genomics Institute with the Stanford OVAL lab.

5
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:25,450
You are seeing interim results, for internal review.

6
00:00:26,762 --> 00:00:28,349
Here is the problem.

7
00:00:28,349 --> 00:00:35,673
When a child with a rare disease is diagnosed, the responsible variant is often reported once, in a case report,

8
00:00:35,673 --> 00:00:38,549
and never submitted to the public databases.

9
00:00:38,550 --> 00:00:42,900
A laboratory testing the next child searches ClinVar and finds nothing.

10
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:50,689
For Alexander disease alone, a 2024 review counts 182 causative variants in 550 published patients,

11
00:00:50,689 --> 00:00:56,512
but ClinVar holds 76 pathogenic or likely pathogenic records for the gene.

12
00:00:56,512 --> 00:01:01,712
The knowledge exists; it is scattered across papers no database can query.

13
00:01:03,026 --> 00:01:08,751
So the question is whether a language model, reading with strict rules, can do the first pass.

14
00:01:08,751 --> 00:01:11,126
Two rules make the answer checkable.

15
00:01:11,126 --> 00:01:19,576
First, every value the model records must carry a verbatim quote from the paper, and a script confirms the quote is really there.

16
00:01:19,576 --> 00:01:25,939
Second, the databases we want to fill, ClinVar and OMIM, are never shown to the model.

17
00:01:25,939 --> 00:01:28,288
They are held out as the answer key.

18
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:34,726
And the whole analysis plan was frozen before the main run, so what you see is what we said we would report.

19
00:01:36,026 --> 00:01:37,263
This is the workflow.

20
00:01:37,263 --> 00:01:41,413
One model reader per paper fills a structured form with quotes.

21
00:01:41,413 --> 00:01:43,601
A script checks every quote.

22
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:51,401
A second model pass, which we call the gatekeeper, re-reads each value against its quote and drops the ones it cannot support.

23
00:01:51,401 --> 00:01:57,763
The result is one standard record per individual, a GA4GH phenopacket.

24
00:01:57,763 --> 00:02:04,161
Below the firewall, a separate harness compares the records with ClinVar, OMIM and two published catalogues,

25
00:02:04,161 --> 00:02:07,064
and lists the variants the databases are missing.

26
00:02:07,064 --> 00:02:12,551
The orange box is what has not happened yet: the human audit and the expert adjudication.

27
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:19,687
We ran six diseases, chosen to span inheritance modes and organ systems.

28
00:02:19,687 --> 00:02:28,612
A20 haploinsufficiency was the development disease, so its numbers are flagged and the other five are the confirmatory set.

29
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:37,625
Only open-access papers were read, which matters later: for most of these diseases, only a fifth to a third of the literature is open.

30
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:40,217
The headline numbers.

31
00:02:40,217 --> 00:02:50,730
350 papers became 2,836 individual records, 1,793 of them carrying a variant in the disease gene.

32
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,967
Every one of 241,730 quotes was found verbatim in its paper.

33
00:02:56,967 --> 00:03:01,392
466 distinct variants could be placed on the genome.

34
00:03:01,392 --> 00:03:05,367
214 of them are in neither ClinVar nor OMIM.

35
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:11,505
And we recovered 43 of the 75 variants OMIM lists for these diseases.

36
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:17,705
First finding: the receipts hold, but the decisive check has not happened.

37
00:03:17,705 --> 00:03:26,380
The gatekeeper judged 0.43 percent of values unsupported across the five confirmatory diseases.

38
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:27,493
That is the blue.

39
00:03:27,493 --> 00:03:34,030
The orange is what human raters found in the pilot, before the pipeline was frozen: around six percent.

40
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:38,180
The machine's reading of its own errors is far below the humans'.

41
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:44,418
The pre-registered test is a blinded audit of 250 claims by two panel members.

42
00:03:44,418 --> 00:03:47,443
It is built, and nobody has rated a claim yet.

43
00:03:47,443 --> 00:03:50,780
Until then, no accuracy claim can be made.

44
00:03:52,082 --> 00:03:54,819
Second finding: we find what we can read.

45
00:03:54,819 --> 00:03:58,975
Against OMIM's listed variants we recovered 57 percent overall,

46
00:03:58,975 --> 00:04:06,032
from nearly ninety percent for the diseases whose literature is open to eleven percent for Tangier disease,

47
00:04:06,032 --> 00:04:09,857
whose variants come from closed papers of the early 2000s.

48
00:04:09,857 --> 00:04:15,932
Of the 84 papers OMIM cites as the sources for its variants, we could read 5.

49
00:04:15,932 --> 00:04:20,232
Where we had read the source paper, the variant was usually in our records.

50
00:04:20,232 --> 00:04:23,394
Access, not reading, sets the ceiling.

51
00:04:24,698 --> 00:04:27,961
Third finding: the database gap is real.

52
00:04:27,961 --> 00:04:38,323
Of 466 genome-placed variants, 245 are in ClinVar, and 168 of those are classified pathogenic or likely pathogenic.

53
00:04:38,323 --> 00:04:48,100
221 are absent from ClinVar; after the checks, 214 remain as candidates, each with an evidence dossier listing every paper,

54
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,723
every quote and every individual.

55
00:04:50,723 --> 00:04:57,273
53 more sit in ClinVar as uncertain or conflicting while the papers call them pathogenic.

56
00:04:57,273 --> 00:05:00,336
Nothing is submitted without expert adjudication.

57
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:07,934
Fourth finding: a cheaper open-weight model finds the same variants and loses the receipts.

58
00:05:07,934 --> 00:05:17,046
On identical inputs, DeepSeek found the same variants about 89 to 95 percent of the time at roughly one 15th of the cost.

59
00:05:17,046 --> 00:05:28,239
But it left 3.0 to 3.6 percent of its values with no quote at all, and the gatekeeper rejected 6.14 to 8.64 percent of its claims,

60
00:05:28,239 --> 00:05:30,822
against Claude's 0.20 to 0.38.

61
00:05:32,138 --> 00:05:33,263
Two more checks.

62
00:05:33,263 --> 00:05:44,013
Running a tenth of the papers twice gave the same checklist answers 97 to 100 percent of the time and the same variants 83 to 100 percent of the time.

63
00:05:44,013 --> 00:05:52,138
And a memorization probe on 180 papers found no sign that either model recalled the papers from training rather than reading them.

64
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:54,475
What does this mean?

65
00:05:54,475 --> 00:06:02,323
On accuracy: the pipeline produces records with complete provenance, but a human-rated error rate is the one number it lacks,

66
00:06:02,323 --> 00:06:05,525
and the audit that provides it costs no model time.

67
00:06:05,525 --> 00:06:12,675
On coverage: a decision on access to paywalled papers would change these results more than any change to the model.

68
00:06:12,675 --> 00:06:20,595
On the answer key: databases curated by people deserve the same scrutiny; OMIM caught one of our normalization errors,

69
00:06:20,595 --> 00:06:25,025
and we found two OMIM entries linked to the wrong ClinVar records.

70
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:27,605
This is where you come in.

71
00:06:27,605 --> 00:06:34,293
We need two raters for the 250-claim audit, roughly ten to fifteen hours each, and a tie-breaker.

72
00:06:34,293 --> 00:06:39,980
We need experts to adjudicate the 214 candidates from their dossiers.

73
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:45,293
We need people to fill four comparator sheets from natural-history cohorts.

74
00:06:45,293 --> 00:06:48,805
And we need reviewers for the methods and the paper.

75
00:06:48,805 --> 00:06:53,943
The dashboard has a short form where you can tell us who you are and how you would like to contribute.

76
00:06:55,250 --> 00:06:56,013
Thank you.

77
00:06:56,013 --> 00:07:03,100
Everything in this overview, the papers, the deck, the candidate list and the form, is on the dashboard.

78
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:10,525
The numbers all trace to files in the project repository, and the report says plainly which questions are still open.

79
00:07:10,525 --> 00:07:12,225
We look forward to working with you.
